From ef158eb319a95c174fab6627065df0ff93563210 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jared Wyles <jared.wyles@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 8 Nov 2026 20:18:33 +1100
Subject: [PATCH 30/32] llama: zero KV pool cells as the high-water mark
 advances, not at creation

Clearing the whole pool at creation makes every page resident before the
first request: with four lanes at the default context that is 48 GiB charged
to the process for cells that may never be used. Attention reads only the
cells get_n_kv() covers, so clear_cells_through zeroes up to that padding
boundary above the highest cell each ubatch writes, once, and a page below
the mark holds zeros or data.

The pool is still cleared at creation when a cell range is not one byte
range of each tensor (a transposed V) or a buffer cannot zero a tensor range
(ggml_backend_buffer_has_memset; ggml_backend_tensor_memset asserts
otherwise). A cache that shares another's cells applies no ubatch itself, so
it has no mark: it skips the creation clear only on Metal, whose fresh
buffers read as zeros, and is cleared at creation everywhere else.
clear_eagerly() restores llama.cpp's behaviour for a caller that wants it.

llama_memory_bufs::emplace gains a clear flag so an owner can zero its
buffers itself.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.2 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01QA3dWey3RBtBgDz1Tery9Y
